**MinMaxStack** — tracks both minimum and maximum in O(1)

```java
class MinMaxStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int minSoFar;
        int maxSoFar;
    }

    private Stack<Node> stack;

    public MinMaxStack() {
        stack = new Stack<>();
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        Node node = new Node();
        node.value = value;

        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            node.minSoFar = value;
            node.maxSoFar = value;
        } else {
            Node prev = stack.peek();
            node.minSoFar = Math.min(value, prev.minSoFar);
            node.maxSoFar = Math.max(value, prev.maxSoFar);
        }

        stack.push(node);
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.pop().value;
    }

    public int getMin() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.peek().minSoFar;
    }

    public int getMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.peek().maxSoFar;
    }
}
```

**Why this design:** By storing cumulative min/max at each node rather than maintaining separate auxiliary stacks, we eliminate redundancy and keep all three operations (push, pop, getMin, getMax) truly O(1) with O(n) space.

