**Implementation: `MinMaxStack`**

I'll implement a stack that tracks both **minimum AND maximum** in O(1) — simultaneously surprising and maximally useful.

```java
public class MinMaxStack {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        int min;
        int max;
        Node next;

        Node(int value, int min, int max) {
            this.value = value;
            this.min = min;
            this.max = max;
        }
    }

    private Node head;

    public void push(int value) {
        int newMin = head == null ? value : Math.min(value, head.min);
        int newMax = head == null ? value : Math.max(value, head.max);

        Node node = new Node(value, newMin, newMax);
        node.next = head;
        head = node;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (head == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        int value = head.value;
        head = head.next;
        return value;
    }

    public int getMin() {
        if (head == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return head.min;
    }

    public int getMax() {
        if (head == null) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return head.max;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return head == null;
    }
}
```

**How it works:**

Each node caches the min and max of **all elements below it** (including itself). When you push, compute new min/max by comparing the incoming value against the previous top's cached min/max. When you pop, discard the node — the next node already has the correct min/max for the remaining stack.

**Complexity:**
- `push(int)`: O(1) — single comparison and node insertion
- `pop()`: O(1) — pointer update
- `getMin()` / `getMax()`: O(1) — read from head node

**Example:**
```
Push 5:    head = [val:5, min:5, max:5]
Push 2:    head = [val:2, min:2, max:5] → [val:5, min:5, max:5]
Push 8:    head = [val:8, min:2, max:8] → [val:2, min:2, max:5] → ...
getMin()   → 2 ✓
getMax()   → 8 ✓
Pop()      → 8, head now points to [val:2, min:2, max:5]
getMax()   → 5 ✓
```
